A||||1||batboy||2.5 hesitation
Z||000000||batboy||07-24-99||01:41 PM||||I'm helping a friend of mine with a 2.5 engine in an 1988 Olds in trying to track down a slight hesitation. It's most pronounced when you suddenly let up on the gas and then try to accelerate again. He has already replaced a bad EGR valve and a marginal oxygen sensor which helped some (the hesitation used to be lots worse). The he replaced the coils, plugs and wire, which then improved gas mileage back to where it used to be...but still there is that slight hesitation. What about the MAP sensor? If it was bad, what does it do? There are no more ECM codes after replacing the EGR and oxygen sensor. We have looked numerous times for vacuum leaks but can't find any. We ran injector cleaner through it too. What are we missing?
Z||000001||theogre||07-24-99||09:24 PM||||MAP, Manifold Absolute Pressure.<p>This device monitors manifold vacuum. Part of it's job is used to produce a "vacuum advance curve" similar to the vac advance on the distributor in a carborated motor. It, or a leak in it's vac line, could cause odd problems. but then a vac leak anywhere can cause odd problems...........<p>It could also be a problem with the TPS. After all these years it's a good idea to replace this item no mater how good it looks.
